Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Euclidean Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Euclidean infrageometry is synthetic geometry: constructions with natural \
objects (points, segments, lines, circles) in a discrete substrate \
represented by a graph ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["G", "TI"], "=", 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["V", "TI"], ",", 
    StyleBox["E", "TI"]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "12619ad1-22bc-4c02-a026-fb88cd88abd7"],
 ". Riemannian infrageometry, in contrast, measures and describes the \
substrate itself."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "GuideFunctionsSection",
 CellID->1866139230,ExpressionUUID->"a0ea3657-4c83-4862-93c8-ecf80cebc774"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Classical geometry has infinitesimality: a segment can be subdivided \
infinitely many times, whatever the scale of the observer. A graph has no \
infinitely small part. Infrageometry aggregates instead. An observer scale ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["r", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "b23b9860-fe07-4f53-931d-6ef1db20ec8f"],
 ", the number of steps the observer inspects at once, must be given before \
an object or a measurement makes sense."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"ba31bc54-2924-46fe-a61b-f21967c2b9ff"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The claim of infrageometry is that limits of graphs are enough to capture \
the geometry of a surface, although a graph describes only points and a web \
of paths between them. This is close to Riemann's original approach. Riemann \
looked for the infinitesimal object that captures the essence of measuring \
the length of a curve, and found the line element ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["s", "TI"], "2"], "=", 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], 
    StyleBox[
     RowBox[{"i", "j"}], "TI"]], 
   StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["i", "TI"]], 
   StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["j", "TI"]]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3c8362bc-2304-4e93-a49e-2178f30a0450"],
 "."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"8267b169-3f84-454f-bbbb-028be693e109"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In synthetic geometry on a graph a construction is not unique; it is \
multi-valued. Two vertices are in general joined by many shortest paths, and \
so they have many midpoints. We deal with this by enumerating the objects a \
construction gives (points in dimension 0, paths in dimension 1) and \
attaching to the construction its vertex density ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], ":", 
    StyleBox["V", "TI"], "\[Rule]", "{", "0"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\[Ellipsis]", "}"}]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ebc2b950-2b47-4cd3-9c68-7b9eb0e3afb7"],
 ", where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   StyleBox["v", "TI"], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "9b11fdcd-8928-4345-b42c-7d977f58afe3"],
 " is the number of objects through the vertex ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["v", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "198430b7-4ec8-467a-bb3c-0a0bb9036526"],
 ". Let graphs converge to a space, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["V", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], ",", 
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], ",", 
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["\[Mu]", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1], "\[Rule]", 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["M", "TI"], ",", 
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], ",", 
    StyleBox["\[Mu]", "TI"]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "40262dd0-593a-44de-afd7-31b18ddf979a"],
 ", in the Gromov-Hausdorff sense for metric measure spaces, with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "78fbdfbd-84d3-4da8-b0ef-30fe36dd1b17"],
 " the rescaled path metric and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["\[Mu]", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "53065709-f2ce-4eb9-b40e-0540cc367ee7"],
 " the rescaled counting measure. We imagine that the normalized densities ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "/", 
   UnderscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
    StyleBox["v", "TI"],
    LimitsPositioning->False], 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["\[Rho]", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   StyleBox["v", "TI"], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2bde19de-b768-40ff-aaa3-581fb53cf48c"],
 " then converge weakly to the Dirac density of the corresponding object in \
the infinitesimal world, concentrated on a point or along a curve."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"c9a1b4eb-b3a0-4604-89ef-6bccf33819b4"],

Cell["\<\
Euclidean infrageometry is synthetic geometry of Euclidean-like objects in \
graphs. We provide a language for elementary multi-valued geometry at the \
infra-scale, and tools to study its convergence to the classical idealized \
\"infinitesimal\" Euclidean geometry, with \"breadthless lines\", unique \
intersections and the like. We represent synthetic objects by inert atomic \
tokens. They can be used for symbolic work, and their evaluation in a \
concrete graph is postponed.\
\>", "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"6f56c5ca-26c4-4268-a09e-cac22e820f4f"],

Cell["\t", "GuideDelimiter",ExpressionUUID->"b35cb19a-d7fb-4b0b-a58a-f092094460a9"],

